                               THE PLAN



   They would hold the story under embargo until ten-thirty on the
thirteenth. If the night went badly, they’d publish what they could
prove. If it went well, they’d publish the same. They didn’t care about
the stones. They cared about a date on an observation report, a ledger,
and a column of signatures, and they had asked her to cross two sen‐
tences out of her summary herself, because a café that stayed peaceful
while a bodega didn’t was an interesting anecdote and not a proof.
   She’d crossed them out. It cost her something. I watched her put the
pen down slowly.
   “They want the raw recordings regardless,” she said. “A flawed ob‐
servation can still point a reporter toward the right question.”
   Vanessa had chosen to attach her name to her own assignments.
Mamá would talk about the letter she had kept in her dresser since
2007. Doña Luz asked for time, and we gave it. Mikey’s file stayed out
of the package entirely: we held his consent for the instruction sheet
and not for his life.
   “What if the night gets canceled?” I asked.
   “They publish.”
   “And if it doesn’t?”
   “Same answer.” She sat on the café’s front step and ate a sandwich
she’d been carrying since noon. “I need somebody else to make the
next hard phone call.”
   “I’ll call my grandmother.”
   She chewed.
   “That’s worse,” she said. “Go ahead.”



Abuela answered from the neighbor’s kitchen with the phone held a
foot from her face, and gave me three instructions before I’d said hello.
   “Primero, pregunta.” First, ask. “She answers whoever asks, and she
answers truly. So you ask before they do.”
   “Okay.”
   “Segundo, nunca las dos juntas.” Never both together. “Not in the
same bag, not in the same pair of hands.”




